the label into this artifact.

00:30:11.113 --> 00:30:12.530
So what happened
behind the scenes

00:30:12.530 --> 00:30:15.050
is I actually deployed
this other thing, way

00:30:15.050 --> 00:30:17.330
before the dog, and
it's called a function.

00:30:17.330 --> 00:30:19.160
In Google Cloud, we
have cloud function,

00:30:19.160 --> 00:30:22.070
so you can actually write a
very small snippet of code

00:30:22.070 --> 00:30:23.700
and it can respond to events.

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:25.510
The event can be
coming from HTTP

00:30:25.510 --> 00:30:28.640
or it can be coming from things
like Google Cloud Storage,

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:31.400
so that when you upload a
picture, or any of the files,

00:30:31.400 --> 00:30:33.570
we can trigger this
function as well.

00:30:33.570 --> 00:30:37.730
If you are using Firestore or
some other things that we have,

00:30:37.730 --> 00:30:39.930
you can also receive
notifications

00:30:39.930 --> 00:30:41.580
when you make changes.

00:30:41.580 --> 00:30:43.650
So I'm going to go ahead
and go back to my IDE.

00:30:43.650 --> 00:30:46.230
And I actually opened up
the function right here.

00:30:46.230 --> 00:30:48.800
So this is a function
that's written with Kotlin.

00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:51.470
And function can run
this code is fine.

00:30:51.470 --> 00:30:54.230
And one thing you have to
watch out for is that you do

00:30:54.230 --> 00:30:55.860
need to actually create a class.

00:30:55.860 --> 00:30:58.523
And then you wrap the actual
function inside of the class.

00:30:58.523 --> 00:31:00.440
That's because we actually
need to instantiate

00:31:00.440 --> 00:31:02.767
the class for you, so
that we can initialize

00:31:02.767 --> 00:31:04.850
some of the client libraries
that you may actually

00:31:04.850 --> 00:31:06.020
have to use.

00:31:06.020 --> 00:31:09.920
And this function is
going to take in an event.

00:31:09.920 --> 00:31:11.900
For example, if it's
HTTP, we're going

00:31:11.900 --> 00:31:14.450
to have some kind of HTTP
request and response.

00:31:14.450 --> 00:31:17.360
In our case, we're going to
respond to a Google Cloud

00:31:17.360 --> 00:31:20.360
Storage event, which is a
well-defined schema that you

00:31:20.360 --> 00:31:22.340
can read the documentation for.

00:31:22.340 --> 00:31:25.550
But you actually definitely
have these two fields in there.

00:31:25.550 --> 00:31:27.020
It's really a JSON format.

00:31:27.020 --> 00:31:29.810
And we're going to serialize
it for you into your data class

00:31:29.810 --> 00:31:30.690
as well.

00:31:30.690 --> 00:31:33.117
So in this function, we
have applyVisionLabel,

00:31:33.117 --> 00:31:34.700
that's going to
receive the event that

00:31:34.700 --> 00:31:38.000
has the bucket and also the
file name that's been changed.

00:31:38.000 --> 00:31:40.670
And we basically just
use a Cloud Vision API

00:31:40.670 --> 00:31:45.350
to read this file and see
what's actually inside.

00:31:45.350 --> 00:31:47.050
And so here we
are actually doing

00:31:47.050 --> 00:31:48.740
a feature, a label detection.

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:51.080
We're going to only get
the maximum of 3 results.

00:31:51.080 --> 00:31:53.960
And then, for the labels,
we're going to just iterate

00:31:53.960 --> 00:31:55.940
through the actual result list.

00:31:55.940 --> 00:31:58.087
We're going to sort by
descending by the score.

00:31:58.087 --> 00:32:00.170
And then we're going to
map it to the description.

00:32:00.170 --> 00:32:01.730
And finally, we're
going to add it

00:32:01.730 --> 00:32:05.020
as a delimited list,
just a string that's

00:32:05.020 --> 00:32:06.710
been delimited by a comma.

00:32:06.710 --> 00:32:09.582
And this is all happening behind
the scenes asynchronously.

00:32:09.582 --> 00:32:11.540
And the way that you
would deploy this function

00:32:11.540 --> 00:32:12.980
is pretty straightforward.

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:15.680
Actually, if I go to here--

00:32:15.680 --> 00:32:18.950
Actually, you know what, I'm
going to open the Read Me here,

00:32:18.950 --> 00:32:20.970
so it's a little
bit easier to read.

00:32:20.970 --> 00:32:22.490
So if I want to
deploy the function,

00:32:22.490 --> 00:32:25.610
I can just say gcloud
function, glcoud [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:25.610 --> 00:32:27.050
gcloud function deploy.

00:32:27.050 --> 00:32:28.890
And then give it the
name of the project.

00:32:28.890 --> 00:32:31.490
And also the entry point,
which is the class name.

00:32:31.490 --> 00:32:33.350
And also the metonym.

00:32:33.350 --> 00:32:35.720
And then you can specify
the event trigger

00:32:35.720 --> 00:32:37.020
that you want to listen to.

00:32:37.020 --> 00:32:39.365
So, for our case, we're
listening to the Google Cloud

00:32:39.365 --> 00:32:41.510
Storage object event trigger.

00:32:41.510 --> 00:32:44.300
And that is how we then
run the Vision API.

00:32:44.300 --> 00:32:45.800
And then we update the label.

00:32:45.800 --> 00:32:49.280
And, of course, if I
go to my console here--

00:32:49.280 --> 00:32:52.695
Let me go back to my IDE.

00:32:52.695 --> 00:32:53.820
Or actually, you know what?

00:32:53.820 --> 00:32:55.300
