00:34:49.650 --> 00:34:51.858
And what we see here, there's
another feature of Ktor

00:34:51.858 --> 00:34:54.570
that we're using, which
is nested routing.

00:34:54.570 --> 00:34:56.460
So here, before, where
we were saying just

00:34:56.460 --> 00:34:58.560
like a GET in the
path, you can actually

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:00.700
have even nested routes in Ktor.

00:35:00.700 --> 00:35:03.330
So I define a route,
and then /all.

00:35:03.330 --> 00:35:06.390
And, in this case, all I'm
doing is querying the data store

00:35:06.390 --> 00:35:08.070
and then saying call.respond.

00:35:08.070 --> 00:35:11.370
Now, this is automatically
serializing this to JSON.

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:13.500
The problem is,
as the log shows,

00:35:13.500 --> 00:35:18.150
is that I forgot to tell Ktor
that I want the JSONs content

00:35:18.150 --> 00:35:20.070
negotiation feature.

00:35:20.070 --> 00:35:21.810
And the way that you
do that, is that you

00:35:21.810 --> 00:35:24.420
come to your
application, and you

00:35:24.420 --> 00:35:27.900
say install(ContentNegotiation)
and then

00:35:27.900 --> 00:35:30.300
the content negotiation has
support for different types

00:35:30.300 --> 00:35:31.320
of serializers.

00:35:31.320 --> 00:35:34.260
So I could say,
for instance, GSON.

00:35:34.260 --> 00:35:37.780
And then GSON has its
own set of parameters,

00:35:37.780 --> 00:35:41.160
so I could say pretty
printer, setPrettyPrinting.

00:35:45.030 --> 00:35:48.120
And because this is GSON, it
knows exactly what GSON has.

00:35:48.120 --> 00:35:50.740
So we have support for GSON
and Jackson, et cetera.

00:35:50.740 --> 00:35:52.530
And that's what the problem was.

00:35:52.530 --> 00:35:57.090
I was not telling Ktor how to
do the actual serialization.

00:35:57.090 --> 00:35:59.280
